**Policy Document: Curriculum and Accreditation Procedures**

**Effective Date:** January 15, 2024  
**Review Date:** January 15, 2026  
**Document Number:** CA-2024-003

**1. Definitions**  
- **Accreditation**: The process by which the Accreditation Council for Education in Medicine (ACEM) evaluates and approves the University of Springfield’s medical program based on established standards.  
- **Curriculum**: The comprehensive educational framework developed by the Curriculum Development Committee (CDC) at the University of Springfield, outlining required courses for degrees in Engineering and Business Administration.

**2. Rules and Procedures**  
- **Compliance**: All academic programs, including those at the Springfield School District (SSD), must align with the curriculum standards set forth by the National Association of Schools of Art and Design (NASAD) and be subject to review every two years.  
- **Student Services**: The Student Services Department, led by Director Jane Doe, will provide resources in collaboration with the Counseling Center of Springfield (CCS) to support student mental health and academic performance metrics.  
- **Evaluation**: The effectiveness of the curriculum will be assessed semi-annually by the Academic Affairs Committee (AAC), which includes representatives from the Faculty Senate and student representatives from the University of Springfield.  

**3. Responsibilities**  
- The Office of Institutional Research will collect and analyze data regarding student outcomes and report findings to the Board of Trustees by March 30 each year.  
- The Dean of Academic Affairs, Dr. John Smith, is responsible for ensuring that all programs maintain their accreditation status with ACEM and NASAD, requiring submission of annual reports detailing student performance and curriculum updates.  
- Faculty members are required to submit course syllabi to the CDC for review at least 30 days before the semester starts, ensuring compliance with accreditation standards.

**4. Interdependencies**  
- The successful accreditation of the University of Springfield’s programs is contingent upon the timely submission of data by the Office of Institutional Research, which relies on accurate input from faculty and Student Services.  
- Collaboration between the CDC and the Office of Student Services is essential to update the curriculum in response to changing industry standards and student needs, facilitating continuous improvement.

